Dan Anderson wrote:
> 1.  I end  up falling  into /  running into a  large pack  of monsters
>     which hit me all at once and kill me. 

You have to learn to use corridors to control packs and don't forget
about your emergency supply of escape items (you have been engraving
wands to identify, right? Keep digging, teleporting, fire, lightning
and the obvious ones, such as wishing, at least) or Elbereth (that's
what the fire and lightning wands are for).

> 2.  I run  out of power  and can't cast  any spells (I usually  play a
>     wizard) and get kiled. 

Spellcasting roles are harder for most beginners to play and, if you
must play them, relying too much on spells can get you killed.  Many
people here swear by a stack of daggers for throwing.

> 3.  Something bad happens in the game -- i.e. I get my armor stolen by
>     a nymph

Nymphs are generated sleeping four times out of five.  Be quieter in
your trek through the dungeons (by not breaking down doors and such)
to avoid waking them.  If you do encounter one, deal with it quickly
and from a distance.

> or I get cursed and can't do anything

You get `cursed'? Do you mean paralysis? Have you been fighting hand
to hand with floating eyes? Don't.  (Actually, it's okay if you take
special precautions but, as you say you play wizards, the best is to
cast spells or throw things at them.)

> or I am lucky enough to use / wear something that is cursed. 

That's not bad luck; your pet won't normally step on things that are
cursed and will indicate those that are when it does.

> 4.  I do something dumb (like break open a wand of invisibility to see
>     what it does, and die. 

Well, at least you learn something from this sort of death.  You may
find that doing it in explore mode wastes less characters, though.

